WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 28: The Comstock Lode and the Great Sutro Tunnel

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:17.520
[Rebecca] In 1859, the discovery of the legendary Comstock load beneath Mount Davidson in western Nevada ignited the greatest silver rush in American history.

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:26.320
[Rebecca] Over $300 million in silver and gold bullion poured from the hills, creating the roaring, high-tech metropolis of Virginia City.

5
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:42.800
[Rebecca] But as miners dug deeper into the heart of the mountain, they punched directly into a subterranean hell, scalding underground geysers of 150 degree water, toxic sulfurous gases, and cave-ins that threatened to drown and cook hundreds of men alive.

6
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:59.360
[Rebecca] Standing against the all-powerful Bank of California monopoly that ruled the territory, a stubborn, charismatic Prussian immigrant named Adolf Sutrow proposed an outrageous engineering gamble, bore a four-mile tunnel through solid rock straight into the mountain's roots.

7
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.160
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

8
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:24.240
[George] Built precariously on the steep eastern slope of Mount Davidson, the city sat directly atop the Comstock load, an immense, complex vein of high-grade gold and silver ore that stretched for miles beneath the earth.

9
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:34.160
[George] Millions of dollars poured daily into the coffers of San Francisco financiers, transforming sleepy California banks into global financial powerhouses.

10
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:42.400
[George] But beneath the glittering surface of the opera houses and champagne bars, the Comstock mines were plunging into a subterranean nightmare.

11
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:49.040
[George] The volcanic geology of the Great Basin meant that the deeper the shafts descended, the hotter the rock became.

12
00:01:49.040 --> 00:02:04.120
[George] At 1,500 feet underground, rock temperatures soared past 130 degrees Fahrenheit, and massive subterranean reservoirs of scalding mineral water at 150 degrees burst through shaft walls with terrifying force.

13
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:15.880
[George] Miners worked stripped completely naked, packing ice against their chests and breathing through canvas air tubes, capable of swinging a pick for only 15 minutes before collapsing from heat prostration.

14
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:32.440
[George] Immense steam pumps costing millions of dollars were installed to pump millions of gallons of boiling water to the surface daily, but when pumps failed or coal ran out, shafts flooded instantly, threatening to turn the entire Comstock load into a watery, abandoned tomb.

15
00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.400
[George] Enter Adolf Heinrich Joseph Suttro.

16
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:47.800
[George] Born in Prussia to a prosperous Jewish cloth manufacturer, Suttro was a brilliant, self-taught engineer who had emigrated to America in 1850 and operated successful stamping mills in Nevada.

17
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:57.080
[George] Sutro looked at the chaotic, dangerous, and wildly expensive pumping operations in Virginia City and recognized a glaring fundamental flaw.

18
00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:04.600
[George] Pumping millions of gallons of boiling water thousands of feet up vertical shafts to the top of a mountain was mechanical insanity.

19
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:09.400
[George] Instead, Suttro proposed an audacious horizontal drainage tunnel.

20
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:25.240
[George] Starting down in the Carson River Valley four miles east of Virginia City, Sutro would bore a straight horizontal tunnel 10 feet high and 12 feet wide, cutting through solid volcanic rock directly into the heart of the Comstock load at a depth of 1600 feet.

21
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.880
[George] The tunnel would serve a triple purpose.

22
00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:36.200
[George] First, it would naturally drain millions of gallons of boiling water by gravity into the Carson River without requiring a single steam pump.

23
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.720
[George] Second, it would act as a massive ventilation flue.

24
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:50.800
[George] Pulling cool desert air deep into the suffocating mine shafts, and third, it would provide a flat, underground rail network to haul heavy ore cars cheaply out to the river mills.

25
00:03:50.800 --> 00:04:04.480
[George] At first, Comstock mine owners embraced Sutrow's vision with enthusiasm, signing contracts in 1866 agreeing to pay a royalty of $2 for every ton of ore extracted once the tunnel drained their mines.

26
00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:11.760
[George] But Sutro's dream soon collided with the most ruthless financial monopoly in the American West, the bank crowd.

27
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:23.120
[George] Controlled by William Sharon and the Bank of California, this syndicate owned the timber flumes, the Virginia and Truckee Railroad, the reduction mills, and the local politicians.

28
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:34.640
[George] Sharon realized that if Sutrow's tunnel succeeded, Sutrow would control the ventilation, drainage, and transportation of the entire Comstock load, shattering the bank's commercial monopoly.

29
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:39.040
[George] Sharon launched a ruthless campaign to destroy the Prussian engineer.

30
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:54.720
[George] The bank pressured mine superintendents to repudiate their contracts, lobbied Congress to kill federal loan guarantees for the tunnel, and spread rumors across Wall Street and San Francisco that Sutrow was a delusional fraud attempting an impossible fantasy.

31
00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.200
[George] Facing total financial ruin, Sutro refused to surrender.

32
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:09.920
[George] He was a man of boundless charisma and ferocious tenacity, blessed with piercing dark eyes, mutton chop whiskers, and a booming voice that could sway an entire auditorium.

33
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.400
[George] Sutro's fortunes turned dramatically following a horrific catastrophe.

34
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:22.800
[George] At dawn on April 7, 1869, a fire broke out at the 800-foot level of the Yellow Jacket Mine in Gold Hill.

35
00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:33.560
[George] Heavy timber supports ignited, and toxic clouds of suffocating wood smoke and deadly carbon monoxides swept through interconnected shafts into the Kentuck and Crown Point mines.

36
00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:44.680
[George] Trapped in a subterranean inferno with no secondary ventilation or escape route, 45 miners suffocated or burned to death in the darkest mining disaster in Nevada history.

37
00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.600
[George] Sutro seized the moment.

38
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:54.760
[George] He rented the Gold Hill Opera House and delivered an impassioned, fiery three-hour speech before thousands of grief-stricken miners.

39
00:05:54.760 --> 00:06:07.880
[George] Showing massive architectural cross-sections of the mountain, Sutro proved that if his horizontal tunnel had been completed, the 45 trapped men could have simply stepped into the tunnel and walked out to safety in fresh air.

40
00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:12.920
[George] Sutro accused the bank ring of sacrificing human lives for corporate dividends.

41
00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.720
[George] They care more for their mules than for you.

42
00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.520
[George] If a mule dies, they must buy another.

43
00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:22.200
[George] If you die, another man stands ready to take your place.

44
00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:30.120
[George] Deeply moved, the miners' union voted overwhelmingly to invest $50,000 of their own union dues into Sutrow's company.

45
00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:39.880
[George] Armed with the miners' backing and private loans raised in London and Paris, Sutrow broke ground on the Great Tunnel on October 19, 1869.

46
00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:49.720
[George] To conquer Mount Davidson's volcanic core, Sutrow realized that traditional hand drilling methods using two-man sledgehammer teams would take 30 years.

47
00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:57.640
[George] Instead, he transformed the Sutro Tunnel into one of the most technologically advanced engineering projects of the 19th century.

48
00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:04.440
[George] Sutro traveled personally to Europe and across the eastern United States to study modern tunneling innovations.

49
00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:13.000
[George] He imported state-of-the-art burley pneumatic rock drills, powered by massive steam-driven air compressors installed at the tunnel mouth.

50
00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:22.080
[George] These compressed air drills hammered into solid granite at 300 strokes per minute, drilling blast holes in a fraction of the time required by hand.

51
00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:34.960
[George] He replaced traditional black powder with the newly invented, highly potent nitroglycerin explosive giant powder, firing blast rounds electrically using insulated wires and battery exploders.

52
00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:49.040
[George] To keep his men alive in the suffocating heat, Sutro constructed giant steam-powered centrifugal blowers that pumped thousands of cubic feet of fresh Carson Valley air through 40-inch galvanized iron pipes directly to the heading face.

53
00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:57.520
[George] Sutro's technological boldness cut years off construction, turning the tunnel into an international marvel of industrial engineering.

54
00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:05.200
[George] Despite these technological advances, the working conditions inside the tunnel heading remained a grueling test of human endurance.

55
00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:13.360
[George] For over three miles, Sutrow's crews bored through treacherous swelling clay, crumbly porphyry, and solid volcanic trachite.

56
00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:21.440
[George] As the heading passed under Mount Davidson, air temperatures soared past 120 degrees with 95% humidity.

57
00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:34.320
[George] Scalding subterranean water at 150 degrees poured from drill holes in the rock face, forcing miners to wear heavy canvas overalls and wrap their hands in thick leather pads to prevent severe blistering.

58
00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:43.440
[George] Four vertical ventilation shafts had to be sunk from the high plateau above, hundreds of feet down into the tunnel alignment, to relieve air pressure.

59
00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:54.000
[George] Sutro himself lived in a ranch house at the mouth of the tunnel, a company town named Sutro, personally inspecting the heading daily and working side by side with his miners.

60
00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:04.600
[George] Month after month, year after year, through bank panics, shareholder lawsuits and physical collapses, the drills hammered incessantly into the dark heart of Mount Davidson.

61
00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:09.880
[George] The historic climax arrived on the evening of July 8, 1878.

62
00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:19.560
[George] At the four-mile mark, deep beneath the town of Virginia City, the miners heard the muffled tapping of miners working in the savage mine shaft just a few feet ahead.

63
00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:25.960
[George] Adolf Sutro himself crawled to the front of the heading, stripped to the waist, sledgehammer in hand.

64
00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:31.880
[George] At nine o'clock in the evening, a final round of dynamite blew away the remaining rock barrier.

65
00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:42.440
[George] With a deafening roar, a torrent of hot air and foul gas rushed through the opening, followed by a tremendous draught of cool, fresh mountain air that swept through the shaft.

66
00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:49.960
[George] Sutro scrambled through the jagged hole, covered in sweat and rock dust, into the arms of the cheering savage miners.

67
00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:58.600
[George] The Great Tunnel was 20,489 feet long, nearly four miles of flawless horizontal alignment through the roots of a mountain.

68
00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:12.680
[George] Although the richest big bonanza ore bodies had largely been exhausted by the time the tunnel was completed, it operated successfully for decades, draining millions of gallons of hot water and proving Sutro's engineering brilliance.

69
00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:24.600
[George] Sutro sold his shares for a massive fortune, moved to San Francisco, invested in vast tracts of oceanfront real estate, and was eventually elected mayor of San Francisco.

70
00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:38.840
[George] Today, the historic portal of the Sutro Tunnel still stands in Dayton, Nevada, a monument to a lone visionary who conquered solid rock, defied financial titans, and carved his name into the bedrock of the West.

71
00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:46.320
[Rebecca] The Comstock load and the Sutro Tunnel remain an astonishing testament to human ingenuity and grit against impossible odds.

72
00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:52.720
[Rebecca] It shows that when vision is backed by unyielding determination, even the heart of a mountain can be unlocked.

73
00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:55.520
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

74
00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:56.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

75
00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:02.800
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we explore the rise and fall of Nevada's ghost city of glass and concrete.

76
00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:05.440
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

